WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.567
-Our next guest
is a prolific writer,

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:04.300
legendary filmmaker
and Academy Award-nominated

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.400
director of more than
70 features and documentaries.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.501
His memoir, "Every Man
for Himself and God Against All"

00:00:10.501 --> 00:00:11.868
is on sale now.

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:14.601
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Werner Herzog.

00:00:14.601 --> 00:00:16.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:23.634
♪♪

00:00:23.634 --> 00:00:31.200
♪♪

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.534
Welcome back, Werner.

00:00:32.534 --> 00:00:34.667
-Thank you.
I like to be back here.

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:35.934
-It is wonderful
to have you here.

00:00:35.934 --> 00:00:38.968
I'm so enjoying
reading your memoir.

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:40.200
But when you write a memoir

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.801
and I have a question
uniquely for you,

00:00:42.801 --> 00:00:46.467
what is Werner Herzog's
relationship with the truth?

00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:50.634
-Well, when you go
into the memoirs,

00:00:50.634 --> 00:00:53.667
you have to verify
what's -- what happened.

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:56.267
So, and some people
are dazed and confused

00:00:56.267 --> 00:01:02.000
because I live such a wild life
as if I had lived five lives.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.534
But I sent the manuscript
to my brothers

00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:07.934
who are very critical,
and they said,

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:11.634
"Yeah, okay, go ahead."
And then people doubt, "Yeah,

00:01:11.634 --> 00:01:13.400
did he move a ship
over a mountain?"

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.133
Yes, I did. It's documented.

00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:16.767
[ Laughter ]

00:01:16.767 --> 00:01:19.501
Did he hypnotize
an entire cast?

00:01:19.501 --> 00:01:24.934
Yes, I did. It's all
in a way verifiable.

00:01:24.934 --> 00:01:30.334
For example, there was doubt --
I staged an opera once in Italy

00:01:30.334 --> 00:01:33.000
and I wanted to have
some stage work

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.834
falling from high up
and through the stage floor.

00:01:36.834 --> 00:01:39.934
And everybody is wondering
what the hell happened here.

00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:43.234
And and the theater
wouldn't give me a stunt man.

00:01:43.234 --> 00:01:47.067
So I tested the stunt myself
from something like...

00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:48.334
[ Audience gasps ]

00:01:48.334 --> 00:01:52.267
Something like 45 feet down
into an air cushion.

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:55.501
And so people say I made it up.
No, I didn't.

00:01:55.501 --> 00:01:57.167
There are a series of photos.

00:01:57.167 --> 00:02:00.367
I can show them to you
or whatever. I was --

00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:03.567
I was shot during an interview.

00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:06.734
It sounds like
"Yes, he made it up."

00:02:06.734 --> 00:02:08.634
No, it's caught on tape.

00:02:08.634 --> 00:02:10.667
It was a BBC interview.

00:02:10.667 --> 00:02:14.467
So you better be careful
when you do something like that.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:17.467
If you write poetry,
you just invent.

00:02:17.467 --> 00:02:20.968
If you write fiction,
you invent completely.

00:02:20.968 --> 00:02:25.200
But here, of course, what is an
inventive element is the style,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.267
the way I'm telling the story.

00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:31.367
The wild sort of content
needs a certain style.

00:02:31.367 --> 00:02:33.934
-And there is, of course,
a lot of documentary evidence

00:02:33.934 --> 00:02:35.901
about the things
you've done either on film

00:02:35.901 --> 00:02:37.267
or just in photographs.

00:02:37.267 --> 00:02:39.701
You chose the photo
for the cover of this book.

00:02:39.701 --> 00:02:41.634
This is you on the edge
of a volcano.

00:02:41.634 --> 00:02:44.067
-Right.
And I had a protective suit.

00:02:44.067 --> 00:02:45.734
-Yes. But I noticed you took off

00:02:45.734 --> 00:02:47.501
this protective part
for the picture.

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:51.234
-Yeah, because it was
at the end of it.

00:02:51.234 --> 00:02:56.834
I was with a volcanologist,
and the volcano was spitting out

00:02:56.834 --> 00:03:01.701
slabs of glowing
hot sort of lava,

00:03:01.701 --> 00:03:04.200
and it came raining down.
You'd better look up.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.367
Where does it come?
You don't duck.

00:03:06.367 --> 00:03:10.200
You have to look and step aside.
So, and we were too close.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.868
And all of a sudden,
some unexpected burst

00:03:13.868 --> 00:03:15.367
and it came raining down.

00:03:15.367 --> 00:03:20.400
And some of these slabs
or blobs,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.968
thousand degrees Celsius,
they come raining down.

00:03:23.968 --> 00:03:27.467
So you better run.
You get out of there.

00:03:27.467 --> 00:03:29.100
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.501
-And I don't mind
to be a coward in such a moment.

00:03:32.501 --> 00:03:34.667
-But it did seem in that moment
you also said,

00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:35.834
"Should we get a picture?"

00:03:35.834 --> 00:03:40.033
[ Laughter ]

00:03:40.033 --> 00:03:43.667
-I don't recall, but no,
I never asked for a picture.

00:03:43.667 --> 00:03:45.801
I'm not so important.

00:03:45.801 --> 00:03:48.801
The film is important
or the writing is important.

00:03:48.801 --> 00:03:51.567
But I had taken off my helmet
because I was sweating

00:03:51.567 --> 00:03:54.701
and I had soot
in my face somehow.

00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:57.133
And actually this photo
was done

00:03:57.133 --> 00:04:00.200
by the volcanologist,
Clive Oppenheimer.

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.100
He had his photo camera
and he said, "Stand still.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:07.200
I take a few photos."
And I kind of like the photos

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:13.000
because my life has been on foot
digging in the jungle

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.467
and moving a ship
and doing things like that.

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:19.968
-You talk about how -- and this
is fascinating to me --

00:04:19.968 --> 00:04:22.667
you can see a whole film before
you start writing it sometimes.

00:04:22.667 --> 00:04:25.868
And you talk about
writing "Aguirre, Wrath of God,"

00:04:25.868 --> 00:04:27.467
which is this movie --

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:29.334
It's hard to imagine
conceiving this whole story

00:04:29.334 --> 00:04:30.467
and sitting down to write it.

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:32.300
But when I pictured
where you wrote it,

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:33.667
I would never have guessed
it was

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:35.634
where you said
you wrote it in the book.

00:04:35.634 --> 00:04:38.767
-Well, it was written
in two and a half days.

00:04:38.767 --> 00:04:43.000
I saw an entire film
and I was on a bus

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:45.734
with my soccer team
and we went to Italy.

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:50.400
We had two huge barrels
of Bavarian beer for our hosts.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.400
We tried to make them drunk
and win the game.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:54.834
So but what happens,

00:04:54.834 --> 00:04:58.834
our team started to open
a bottle -- a whole barrel,

00:04:58.834 --> 00:05:01.100
and they were drunk
within two hours.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:05.033
And I was sitting at that time
a little typewriter on my knees

00:05:05.033 --> 00:05:06.901
and writing the screenplay,

00:05:06.901 --> 00:05:09.400
and even my goalie
leans over me and he says,

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.133
"What are you doing?"
Then vomits over my typewriter.

00:05:12.133 --> 00:05:14.133
[ Laughter ]
So what can you do?

00:05:14.133 --> 00:05:16.734
I threw out a few pages
from the window

00:05:16.734 --> 00:05:19.400
and I forgot what was on it.
I forgot.

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:23.601
So I had to type on
and I wrote the screenplay.

00:05:23.601 --> 00:05:28.400
I don't need a quiet island
where I'm all alone

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:30.234
and things are serene.

00:05:30.234 --> 00:05:33.901
I can write
on a playground for children

00:05:33.901 --> 00:05:37.067
who are throwing toys
at each other and scream

00:05:37.067 --> 00:05:39.267
and the dog is barking at me.

00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:41.868
It doesn't matter.
I can write any place.

00:05:41.868 --> 00:05:44.334
-You wrote something
very funny in the book

00:05:44.334 --> 00:05:46.634
that I would really
like you to do.

00:05:46.634 --> 00:05:49.901
You wrote -- You were talking
about seeing the auctioneers

00:05:49.901 --> 00:05:52.067
at a livestock auction,
and you wrote,

00:05:52.067 --> 00:05:55.167
"I always wanted to direct a
'Hamlet' and have all the parts

00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:57.901
played by ex-champion
livestock auctioneers."

00:05:57.901 --> 00:05:59.200
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.100
"I wanted the performance
to come in at under 14 minutes."

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:03.701
[ Laughter ]
-Yes.

00:06:03.701 --> 00:06:07.267
Well, still -- still
unfinished project.

00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:08.534
[ Laughter ]

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:12.634
And, you know, you know,
these livestock auctioneers,

00:06:12.634 --> 00:06:15.701
they speak with a speed
that is unimaginable.

00:06:15.701 --> 00:06:20.133
For me, the last poetry
of capitalism, so to speak,

00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:22.467
and I really want --
anyone knows

00:06:22.467 --> 00:06:24.901
basically what's happening
in "Hamlet."

00:06:24.901 --> 00:06:27.767
And I want to bring it down
under 14 minutes.

00:06:27.767 --> 00:06:29.434
-Yeah.
-Unfinished business.

00:06:29.434 --> 00:06:30.634
-There you go.
Well, you have a lot.

00:06:30.634 --> 00:06:32.634
And I would like to --

00:06:32.634 --> 00:06:34.901
There's a film you did
that last time you left,

00:06:34.901 --> 00:06:37.167
I so regretted -- and I'm a fan
of so many of your films.

00:06:37.167 --> 00:06:39.968
But "Bad Lieutenant:
Port of Call New Orleans,"

00:06:39.968 --> 00:06:41.234
you mentioned
in the book as well.

00:06:41.234 --> 00:06:45.167
It is again a career
of fine performances.

00:06:45.167 --> 00:06:47.501
It might be my favorite
Nicolas Cage performance.

00:06:47.501 --> 00:06:49.501
And you concur.

00:06:49.501 --> 00:06:54.701
-And Nicolas Cage
had been interviewed about it.

00:06:54.701 --> 00:06:56.767
"What is your favorite
performance?"

00:06:56.767 --> 00:07:00.767
And he says, "There's one,"
"Bad Lieutenant" with me

00:07:00.767 --> 00:07:02.334
and then there's nothing
and nothing

00:07:02.334 --> 00:07:06.767
and then comes the performance
that won him the Academy Award.

00:07:06.767 --> 00:07:08.400
I think "Leaving Las Vegas."
-"Leaving Las Vegas." Yes.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.501
-Yes. And it was so wonderful
to work with him,

00:07:11.501 --> 00:07:15.434
such a -- such a great,
great actor.

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:18.033
And I loved every minute
with him.

00:07:18.033 --> 00:07:19.567
And it was wonderful.

00:07:19.567 --> 00:07:21.300
-Well, it shows up
on the screen.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:22.667
It is a fantastic film.
-It shows.

00:07:22.667 --> 00:07:24.601
-Yes. It's a fantastic book.

00:07:24.601 --> 00:07:26.801
And I know you're
as prolific a writer.

00:07:26.801 --> 00:07:28.167
You've already
written another book,

00:07:28.167 --> 00:07:30.133
which means you get to come
back here again soon.

00:07:30.133 --> 00:07:32.033
-Okay. Thank you.
-Thank you very much.

00:07:32.033 --> 00:07:33.801
Werner Herzog, everybody.

00:07:33.801 --> 00:07:37.334
"Every Man for Himself and God
Against All" available

00:07:37.334 --> 00:07:38.467
wherever you buy your books.

